#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151799 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5462223e-a206-572a-846a-378621922011" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Direct Variation Equation from a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "1a5e8fef-3361-5d24-9abd-d0474a2cd3ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-1141" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6ca63cd3e05b74666259ef1696e51f702f6fb86f770ff84ca3b0ce7c3cd74744.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the relationship between the displacement $d$, in centimeters, of a particular spring when a mass $m$, in kilograms, is attached to it. Find a direct variation equation that describes the relationship between $d$ and $m$." :question/worked-solution "Since $d$ varies directly with $m$, we use the equation\n\n$$\nd = km\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that there is a point on the graph with $m=5$ and $d=20$. So, we substitute this data into the above equation to find $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20 &= k \\cdot 5 \\\\\n\\frac{20}{5} &= k \\\\\n4 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n$d=4m$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151920 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32650c78-38d5-5530-991d-bf0b8f2198cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graphs of Proportional Relationships"}] :question/id #uuid "42e81da3-7ea4-5b58-9163-3057df064c21" :question/math-academy-id "e-4460" :question/problem "Which of the graphs below represent proportional relationships?\n\n![](images/4c/4cc220afc9f6b1f8752076d0b8d8233caec22e937c0579926439b39472be8c80.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the graph of a proportional relationship is always a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0)$.\n\nOf the given graphs, only graph II fits this description.\n\n- Graphs I and IV are not straight lines.\n- Graph III does not pass through the origin $(0,0)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9c2ec6a-e04a-5e97-a91c-3a232e9aa29d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality or Unit Rate"}] :question/id #uuid "06bdefa1-8b40-5777-9ca0-9f257834833d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4461" :question/problem "The graph below shows a proportional relationship. What is the constant of proportionality?\n\n![](images/62/6298e9ee004e642d05d065d1fc2fd325f07d313ac736ef15b190c1b9c90d1019.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(4,3)$.\n\n![](images/d5/d573d9aad231622b2e5dc42a8672d973e5fda518d75894f3bafb0b1a97532d24.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is\n\n$$\nk=\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$"}]}
